---
// /portal/totp-setup — RETIRED. This URL now forwards to the real security screen.
//
// =================================================================================================
// WHY THIS PAGE IS GONE RATHER THAN FIXED IN PLACE
// =================================================================================================
//
// What used to be here looked like an authenticator set-up flow — QR code, backup codes, "Google
// Authenticator set up successfully! TOTP is now active." — and none of it was connected to
// anything that signs anybody in.
//
//   1. IT WROTE A TABLE NO SIGN-IN PATH READS. Every statement on this page touched
//      `user_totp_secrets`. The product's authenticator lives in `user_totp`
//      (src/lib/auth/twofactor.ts), which is what isTotpEnabled(), availableSecondFactors() and
//      verifyLoginCode() read. Outside this page the only mention of user_totp_secrets in the whole
//      repository was a DELETE in the admin console. So a person could complete this flow, be told
//      their account now required a second step, and have exactly the protection they had before —
//      none. A false assurance of security is worse than a missing feature, because it stops the
//      person from turning on the real one.
//
//   2. IT NEVER VERIFIED THE CODE. The `verify_totp` branch tested `code.length === 6 &&
//      /^\d+$/.test(code)` and then set is_active = true. `000000` passed. There was no RFC 6238
//      check anywhere in the file, and the secret it claimed to be confirming was read out of a
//      hidden form field — a caller-supplied value compared against nothing.
//
//   3. IT TURNED THE FACTOR OFF WITH NO PROOF. `remove` cleared is_active for the signed-in user
//      with no current code demanded, and `generate` replaced the stored secret (setting
//      is_active = false) on a bare POST. /api/2fa/disable requires a current code for exactly this
//      reason: a hijacked session must not be able to strip the second step.
//
//   4. EVERY WRITE WAS `.catch(() => {})` UNDER AN UNCONDITIONAL SUCCESS MESSAGE. A write that threw
//      produced the same green sentence as one that landed, and logged nothing.
//
// src/lib/workforce/navigation.ts already recorded this page in NAV_BACKLOG as
// `totp-setup.duplicate` — "a second door into one room, and it is the one nobody links… the fix is
// a DELETION or a redirect, not a nav entry". Nothing in src/ links here, so this removes no
// journey: the working flow is /portal/security (and /portal/employee/security for staff), both of
// which render the shared TwoFactorPanel over the real engine, verify the code server-side, and
// issue recovery codes that are actually accepted at sign-in.
//
// A REDIRECT RATHER THAN A DELETED FILE, because this URL may still be sitting in an old email, and
// a 404 on a security link is how somebody concludes the whole feature was withdrawn. A signed-out
// visitor is sent to sign in first and lands here again, which forwards them on.
const user = Astro.locals.user;
if (!user) return Astro.redirect('/portal/login?next=' + encodeURIComponent('/portal/totp-setup'));

return Astro.redirect('/portal/security', 302);
---
